     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	`solution.py` implements:
     6	
     7	```python
     8	def max_element(l: list):
     9	    return max(l)
    10	```
    11	
    12	The K claims prove partial correctness of the exact translated body.  They
    13	start from module loading, bind `max_element`, call it through MPY's ordinary
    14	name lookup and function-frame rules, evaluate the program-defined body, look
    15	up the fixed `max` builtin, and execute MPY's iterator folds.  The final result
    16	is the corresponding recursive maximum summary, and the claims also require
    17	the final environment, scope allocation counter, heap, heap counter, stack,
    18	return cell, exception cell, and exit code to have their stated values.
    19	
    20	The required target is unbounded.  Four entry claims collectively cover every
    21	nonempty list for which the supplied MPY semantics defines `max`:
    22	
    23	- `SPEC.max-element-int-head`: an integer head and an arbitrary finite
    24	  numeric tail;
    25	- `SPEC.max-element-float-head`: a float head and an arbitrary finite numeric
    26	  tail;
    27	- `SPEC.max-element-bool-head`: a Boolean head and an arbitrary finite
    28	  numeric tail;
    29	- `SPEC-STR.max-element-str-head`: a string head and an arbitrary finite
    30	  string tail.
    31	
    32	Here numeric means any mixture of MPY `Int`, `Bool`, and `Float`.  The recursive
    33	`allNumericVS` and `allStrVS` preconditions do not bound list length.
    34	
    35	The companion circularities are
    36	`SPEC.max-int-numeric-acc`, `SPEC.max-float-numeric-acc`,
    37	`SPEC.max-general-numeric-acc`, and `SPEC-STR.max-general-str-acc`.  Their base
    38	case consumes an empty remainder, their step case consumes one constructor,
    39	and the entry claims instantiate them after the first element seeds MPY's
    40	fold.
    41	
    42	## Formal postcondition
    43	
    44	The numeric claims return `maxIntNumericVS`, `maxFloatNumericVS`, or
    45	`maxNumericGeneralVS` according to MPY's accumulator state.  The string claim
    46	returns `maxStrGeneralVS`.  Each summary recursively keeps the old candidate
    47	unless the next value is strictly greater, so ties retain the first maximal
    48	element, matching Python and the supplied MPY builtin.
    49	
    50	This is a partial-correctness result under the supplied semantics and the
    51	float trust boundary below.  It does not separately prove termination.
    52	
