   140	### Float primitive wrapper
   141	
   142	- Extensions: `maxFOpaque`; concrete
   143	  `maxFOpaque(F1,F2) => maxFloat(F1,F2)`; symbolic
   144	  `maxFloat(F1,F2) => maxFOpaque(F1,F2)`.
   145	- Class: trusted primitive wrapper.
   146	- Semantic role: only K's fixed `FLOAT.max` hook is isolated; no Python or
   147	  MPY program region is skipped.
   148	- Domain and matched context: every symbolic pair of `Float` values in a
   149	  pure term context.  The symbolic rule has no configuration-cell pattern,
   150	  continuation, binding, or control effect.  The concrete rule covers every
   151	  ground pair.
   152	- Context containment/state footprint: `FLOAT.max` is a pure fixed hook, so
   153	  arbitrary pure-term context is its complete context and it reads/writes no
   154	  MPY state cell.
   155	- Value influence: the returned `Float` may be the result or may participate
   156	  in a later fixed float comparison.
   157	- Value justification: conclusions involving it are conditional on the named
   158	  contract `maxFOpaque(F1,F2) = FLOAT.max(F1,F2)`.  The proof only threads the
   159	  term; it does not assert a different value or reuse it as an unconstrained
   160	  postcondition oracle.
   161	- Dependents: `max-float-numeric-acc`, the float-headed entry claim, and
   162	  mixed numeric paths reached after a float accumulator.
   163	- Validation: the Haskell backend's missing `FLOAT.max` evaluator is the
   164	  reason for the wrapper.  LLVM executes the supplied hook on the recorded
   165	  homogeneous and mixed-float smoke cases with no assertion failure.
