   263	## Empirical evidence
   264	
   265	`smoke.py` is an executable CPython oracle with explicit expected results.
   266	The same file is translated to `smoke.mpy` and executed under LLVM MPY.
   267	Both runs exit 0 with zero assertion failures on:
   268	
   269	- the two prompt examples;
   270	- a negative singleton;
   271	- lexicographically ordered strings;
   272	- Booleans;
   273	- mixed int/float/bool values;
   274	- homogeneous floats; and
   275	- an exact mixed int/float comparison above `2^53`.
   276	
   277	This finite evidence supports the fixed primitive boundary; it is not used as
   278	a universal proof.
   279	
   280	## Excluded behavior and model boundaries
   281	
   282	- Empty lists are excluded because there is no maximum; CPython raises
   283	  `ValueError`, and the supplied MPY fold has no successful empty seed.
   284	- Lists mixing strings with numeric values are excluded because Python's
   285	  ordering is undefined and raises `TypeError`.
   286	- CPython orderings not modeled by the supplied fixed semantics, such as
   287	  lexicographic ordering of nested lists/tuples and user-defined comparison
   288	  methods, are a language-model boundary.  The candidate does not replace
   289	  those behaviors; the K theorem makes no claim about them.
   290	- The formal float claims cover MPY `Float` values, but value-level alignment
   291	  for exotic IEEE cases (NaN, infinities, and signed zero) is conditional on
   292	  the supplied fixed float primitives.  No unchecked claim of CPython
   293	  equivalence for those edges is made.
